WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:05.220
Hi everyone my name is Mithuna and I'm a third year PhD student studying quantum

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:09.929
physics at the University of Cambridge. In this video I just wanted to explain

00:00:09.929 --> 00:00:13.889
how I got here
since what I started off in high school

00:00:13.889 --> 00:00:20.640
I really wasn't into science. In fact I
quite liked art. I mean you probably can't

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:25.080
tell from the quality of my illustrations
these days, but back then I wasn't so bad,

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:29.730
and I really enjoyed it and I also
really enjoyed English literature and

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:33.600
learning a second language and basically
I really identified as a humanities student.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:39.420
So what changed and how on earth
did I get here eventually?

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:45.450
So the big turnaround came for me when I
was 16 suddenly I understood what was

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:49.470
going on in science class. Maybe it was
because I had a really great teacher at

00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:53.820
the time or maybe it was because we were learning some really cool topics at

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:58.680
the time, but it made sense for the first
time. I realized that science was about

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:04.559
coming up with basic laws that are
simple and yet can explain so much

00:01:04.559 --> 00:01:10.470
complex phenomena. So then after that I
picked up this popular science book

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:14.039
which you might have heard of. It's by
Bill Bryson and it's called a Short

00:01:14.039 --> 00:01:18.360
History of Nearly Everything. I love that
book and I totally recommend it. When I

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:23.460
finished that book I have this distinct
memory of closing it and thinking I'm

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:28.950
gonna be a physicist. And yet it wasn't
such an easy path. For one thing I was

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:34.110
still absolutely terrible at maths. At
the age of 13 when we start high school

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:39.570
we had taken a test that would show us
whether we were good at maths or not

00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:44.430
apparently, and then according to that we
would end up in different levels of

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:50.579
maths class, and I was placed in one of
the lowest maths levels- in fact almost

00:01:50.579 --> 00:01:54.899
the lowest maths level -and I'd stayed
there for all these years. Suddenly I

00:01:54.899 --> 00:01:57.930
realized that if I wanted to be a
physicist I would have to do quite a lot

00:01:57.930 --> 00:02:02.220
of maths, so I decided that I would take
the highest level of maths that they

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:06.780
offered at my school and I asked my
maths teacher at the time whether he

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:13.170
thought I would be able to do it. He
laughed and walked away... but he couldn't

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:18.090
stop me and so I did it anyway -even
though my teacher the next year really

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:23.190
really tried to persuade me to drop the
class. In my final year though I had a

00:02:23.190 --> 00:02:28.230
fantastic maths teacher and I finally
realized that actually a lot of the

00:02:28.230 --> 00:02:31.709
things that I liked about physics were
the same things that I could like about

00:02:31.709 --> 00:02:38.130
maths - that you can take simple rules
and use logic to be able to solve

00:02:38.130 --> 00:02:44.400
complicated problems. I still struggled a
bit with maths and so it wasn't my very

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:49.319
favourite subject. That was still
definitely physics. At this point I

00:02:49.319 --> 00:02:54.630
remember I often would skip having
lunch with my friends so I could go to the

00:02:54.630 --> 00:02:59.150
library and read physics textbooks.

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:03.540
In my first year of university I did
physics and chemistry along with a

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:08.130
second language and a maths course. The maths course that I picked though wasn't

00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:12.990
the one meant for people who wanted to
become mathematicians, it was the one who

00:03:12.990 --> 00:03:17.880
meant for people who were interested in
engineering, let's say. I decided to do

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:21.720
that course because I was still not
feeling super confident about my

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:26.130
mathematical abilities.
In that course we learned about linear

00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:31.709
algebra which is super beautiful- I love
that you can just visualize what's going on.

00:03:31.709 --> 00:03:37.319
At the same time though I was
starting to feel more and more

00:03:37.319 --> 00:03:44.340
disillusioned with physics. Ironically
the final nail in that coffin was

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:49.709
quantum mechanics in second year. Quantum mechanics was this course that I was

00:03:49.709 --> 00:03:55.050
super excited to do because it was meant
to be about like the strange laws that

00:03:55.050 --> 00:04:01.590
govern physics... and yet quantum mechanics class was mostly about solving this

00:04:01.590 --> 00:04:06.360
partial differential equation for
different boundary conditions. I'd come

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:09.330
to physics because I thought that I'd
learned how to solve problems

00:04:09.330 --> 00:04:14.250
but instead I felt that I was just
learning how to use mathematical

00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:19.209
formulas. At that point I was feeling quite lost

00:04:19.209 --> 00:04:24.389
but I got very lucky because the next
semester I decided to take a course in

00:04:24.389 --> 00:04:31.180
abstract algebra for no other reason
then that it sounded similar to linear

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:35.470
algebra, which I really enjoyed. This
course was meant for mathematicians and

00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:40.810
so in particular it was meant for people
who had taken the hard math courses the

00:04:40.810 --> 00:04:45.570
year before and in the semester before which I hadn't, so I was completely unprepared.

00:04:45.570 --> 00:04:49.000
I took the course anyway and I
ended up loving it.

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:52.690
This was the first time that I'd met
pure maths, and pure maths is all about

00:04:52.690 --> 00:04:58.180
taking axioms that describe a particular
type of system and then from those

00:04:58.180 --> 00:05:04.480
axioms driving lots of properties of
that system that aren't obvious. It

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:08.770
had a lot of these elements that are
really liked about physics but it was

00:05:08.770 --> 00:05:12.010
very very rigorous which was actually
quite a challenge for me, because up

00:05:12.010 --> 00:05:16.720
until this point I was a very intuitive
kind of person. Even in physics I would

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:20.470
give very hand-wavy arguments for things
and I would just think that certain

00:05:20.470 --> 00:05:24.460
things are obviously true and because
it's obvious to me but they're true I

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:29.050
wouldn't need to show that they're true.
Whereas maths is the complete opposite,

00:05:29.050 --> 00:05:33.970
and I really really did struggle because
of that culture clash. But it taught me

00:05:33.970 --> 00:05:40.090
so much and I after that was completely
hooked on the pure maths way of thinking

00:05:40.090 --> 00:05:46.060
and I decided to switch my focus from
physics to maths. The Bachelor of Science

00:05:46.060 --> 00:05:50.740
was very very flexible and so I did a
bunch of other subjects as well. I

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:58.030
decided to take a course in information
theory because thermodynamics has this

00:05:58.030 --> 00:06:02.440
quantity called entropy in it and
information had this quantity called

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:07.360
entropy in it and I was just wondering,
are they the same thing or not? So I did

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:11.410
this class that was meant for computer
scientists I was the only not computer

00:06:11.410 --> 00:06:17.380
scientists in the class and it was so
cool -no matter what discipline you're in

00:06:17.380 --> 00:06:23.260
I would recommend information theory. It
has such deep consequences for basically

00:06:23.260 --> 00:06:29.229
every other subject of human knowledge.
In my final year of undergraduate I took

00:06:29.229 --> 00:06:32.570
a course in functional
elseis which is basically the maths of

00:06:32.570 --> 00:06:38.180
quantum mechanics and it's basically
linear algebra on steroids. I really

00:06:38.180 --> 00:06:43.400
loved that and so I had this crazy idea
that I would become a mathematical

00:06:43.400 --> 00:06:48.620
physicist who focuses on the maths of
quantum mechanics. So basically I would

00:06:48.620 --> 00:06:52.730
become a physicist just to have an
excuse to be able to do maths that

00:06:52.730 --> 00:06:57.710
applies to the 'real world'. I'm so glad
that I didn't end up there and the

00:06:57.710 --> 00:07:04.460
reason I didn't end up there is because
of this channel. For some reason I made

00:07:04.460 --> 00:07:07.250
a couple of videos about quantum
mechanics.

00:07:07.250 --> 00:07:11.930
I made them about quantum mechanics, I
think, just because I thought well I

00:07:11.930 --> 00:07:15.500
understand quantum mechanics. I've been
doing all these courses on quantum

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:20.090
mechanics, surely I could explain it. And
I loved explaining things so I just kind

00:07:20.090 --> 00:07:25.400
of made some videos. Those first two
videos were absolutely shocking and not

00:07:25.400 --> 00:07:31.669
just because of the quality ...although
that is pretty hard to look at now, but

00:07:31.669 --> 00:07:35.780
also because of the content. See, I
decided that these videos wouldn't have

00:07:35.780 --> 00:07:40.669
any maths in them because they were
meant for laypeople but when I have to

00:07:40.669 --> 00:07:44.600
take away the maths
I realized I didn't understand quantum

00:07:44.600 --> 00:07:48.979
mechanics at all. Sure I knew how to
solve the Schrodinger equation and I

00:07:48.979 --> 00:07:54.500
knew what Hilbert space was but, when it
came down to what does quantum mechanics

00:07:54.500 --> 00:08:00.830
actually say about how the world works, I
had no idea. That's why when I finished

00:08:00.830 --> 00:08:05.540
my undergraduate degree I took six
months off and started learning quantum

00:08:05.540 --> 00:08:11.960
mechanics. Like properly learning what it
meant and as I learnt things I made

00:08:11.960 --> 00:08:16.460
videos about them. Most of those videos
are now unlisted but I was actually

00:08:16.460 --> 00:08:21.050
really productive at the time I made it
like 20 videos or something. On this

00:08:21.050 --> 00:08:26.090
journey of learning quantum mechanics
from my bedroom I met Bell's Theorem for

00:08:26.090 --> 00:08:29.900
the first time and started thinking
about the foundations of quantum

00:08:29.900 --> 00:08:34.070
mechanics and hidden variables and
bohmian mechanics and all kinds of crazy

00:08:34.070 --> 00:08:39.380
stuff like that and I just loved it so
much -and that's when I realized this is

00:08:39.380 --> 00:08:43.370
what I want to do. I want to study
quantum mechanics but properly study

00:08:43.370 --> 00:08:46.380
quantum mechanics. Not as a mathematician trying to

00:08:46.380 --> 00:08:53.070
just manipulate symbols, but to actually
understand what was happening. That's how

00:08:53.070 --> 00:08:59.250
I ended up doing a honours (which is sort
of like a one-year masters) in physics

00:08:59.250 --> 00:09:04.440
rather than maths and I had to pick a
subject to do my thesis in and I chose

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:09.149
quantum computing (and information) -not that I knew anything about quantum computing but

00:09:09.149 --> 00:09:13.800
because it had the words quantum and
information which I already told you I

00:09:13.800 --> 00:09:18.149
was very interested in but also
computing which I'd learnt about in a

00:09:18.149 --> 00:09:22.290
course on logic and thought was super
fascinating as well, so I thought the

00:09:22.290 --> 00:09:27.269
combination of these must be cool. And
it turned out I was totally right. Quantum

00:09:27.269 --> 00:09:36.389
computing is a fascinating field. See
quantum computers are computers that use

00:09:36.389 --> 00:09:41.790
the spooky laws of quantum mechanics to do calculations faster than it should be

00:09:41.790 --> 00:09:45.810
possible for a classical computer to be
able to do them. When this was discovered

00:09:45.810 --> 00:09:50.670
people were really surprised because
there are many different ways to build a

00:09:50.670 --> 00:09:55.199
classical computer and yet they all end
up being equivalent -they can all solve

00:09:55.199 --> 00:10:00.899
the same class of problems to the same
sort of efficiency - and so it doesn't

00:10:00.899 --> 00:10:04.769
really matter what kind of model you're
looking at. And yet when you make this

00:10:04.769 --> 00:10:09.630
other model of computing using quantum
mechanics suddenly it does matter and

00:10:09.630 --> 00:10:13.740
the class of computations that you can
do is different and so this means that

00:10:13.740 --> 00:10:19.800
quantum mechanics obeys some
fundamentally different logic to regular

00:10:19.800 --> 00:10:24.510
classical mechanics. So there's this big
open question in quantum computing which

00:10:24.510 --> 00:10:32.160
is why? What exactly is so special about
quantum mechanics? Exactly which part of

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:36.899
quantum mechanics is allowing for the
speed up to happen? That was what I was

00:10:36.899 --> 00:10:42.510
interested in in my honours thesis. I
obviously didn't solve it but I decided

00:10:42.510 --> 00:10:47.430
that I wanted to keep studying this
particular topic and that's why I ended

00:10:47.430 --> 00:10:52.860
up on almost the literal other side of
the world, because my supervisor here in

00:10:52.860 --> 00:10:56.800
Cambridge is one of the people who's
really interested in

00:10:56.800 --> 00:11:01.000
the question and has done a lot of work on it in the past and I wanted to work with

00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:07.330
him, so I applied and somehow ended up
here. So that's my story of how I went

00:11:07.330 --> 00:11:13.240
from someone who thought that she was
terrible at maths and science and ended

00:11:13.240 --> 00:11:19.750
up as a quantum physicist. If I have any
moral or any advice at all it's that I

00:11:19.750 --> 00:11:24.250
want you to be open-minded about the
kind of things that you could enjoy if

00:11:24.250 --> 00:11:30.100
you gave them a go, but also be open to
minded about what you could get good at

00:11:30.100 --> 00:11:34.899
with practice.
So thanks for watching this video this

00:11:34.899 --> 00:11:40.149
is the first time I've had my face on
these YouTube videos and I'm really

00:11:40.149 --> 00:11:45.309
nervous about the comments.. so please
don't leave me anything too weird. My mom

00:11:45.309 --> 00:11:50.109
reads them. I'm just kidding she doesn't
even watch my videos. Anyway you know

00:11:50.109 --> 00:11:55.119
what I mean. Also the video about Hilbert
space and Dirac notation and the maths

00:11:55.119 --> 00:11:59.499
of quantum mechanics- I'm writing it
right now and that means that it should

00:11:59.499 --> 00:12:03.910
be coming out soon which means that it
should be coming out within the next two

00:12:03.910 --> 00:12:08.369
months, probably. Hopefully sooner!

